implementationPlan.description:
Implement the single value-preserving file-JSON escape boundary. In atomicWrite.ts define a module-private regex for EXACTLY U+061C, U+200B–U+200F, U+2028–U+202E, U+2060–U+206F, U+FEFF. To keep source/tool bytes unambiguous, an ASCII-only implementation is:
const fileJsonFormatChars = new RegExp('[' + [[0x061c],[0x200b,0x200f],[0x2028,0x202e],[0x2060,0x206f],[0xfeff]].map(range => range.map(cp => String.fromCharCode(cp)).join('-')).join('') + ']', 'g');
export function escapeJsonForFile(json: string): string { return json.replace(fileJsonFormatChars, ch => String.fromCharCode(92) + 'u' + ch.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).padStart(4, '0')); }
Apply escapeJsonForFile(JSON.stringify(obj,null,2)) in BOTH atomicWriteJson and atomicWriteJsonAsync, before invoking their existing raw-text siblings. Add a concise why-comment: prevent hidden-Unicode/Trojan Source warnings while preserving parsed values. Do not touch raw text helpers, retry/backoff, fsync, cleanup, object mutation or error propagation. In accounts/registry.ts import this stateless helper and wrap only the existing JSON.stringify(value,null,2), preserving the final newline, mode 0600, recursive directory mode 0700, rename retries and thrown errors. This utility has only Node fs/path dependencies and no daemon/service side effects, so accounts remains runnable as a standalone CLI. Do not change transport JSON serialization or canonical digest code.
